1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Selection and projection. Choosing rows, choosing columns, and changing nothing.

2
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:10,700
The lab's four tickets (subjects shortened). Each row is one support request.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,200
ticket_id is the primary key: unique, never NULL. Follow IDs, not positions.

4
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:18,100
NULL here means nobody is assigned yet. The ticket itself is not missing.

5
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:22,500
Selection tests each row against a condition and keeps the rows where it is true.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,500
One row passes. 1003, 1004, and 1009 fail the test...

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,400
...but they are still in the table. Nothing was changed or deleted.

8
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:30,900
If a dashboard used status = 'open', every new request would vanish.

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:39,900
The query runs with no error. It just answers a different question.

10
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:45,400
The condition is where the business rule lives. Write it down, then check it.

11
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:48,500
Projection keeps the attributes you name. There is no row condition here.

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,000
All four tickets remain, each with two columns. The stored table keeps every column.

13
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:57,700
Hiding a column on a screen is not, by itself, security.

14
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,400
Tickets 1001 and 1003 both have 201, so SQL shows 201 twice.

15
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:05,200
A mathematical relation is a set: identical tuples collapse. SQL keeps them.

16
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:12,900
To remove repeats, ask for it with DISTINCT. Neither result has a guaranteed order.

17
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:15,900
Relational selection is the row filter. In SQL it lives in WHERE.

18
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,900
The list after the SELECT keyword is projection.

19
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,900
Together: choose the rows, then choose the columns.

20
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:33,500
Remember: Selection (σ) keeps rows that satisfy a condition: SQL's WHERE. Projection (π) keeps the columns you name: SQL's SELECT list. Neither one changes or deletes stored rows. Valid SQL can still encode the wrong business rule. SQL keeps repeated rows unless you ask for DISTINCT.

